epic: E-EXPORT
wu: E-EXPORT.W6
branch: wu/export/controls-build
changes:
  - path: docs/use-cases/web-ui.html
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: >-
      UC-4 gains a new block, "The capture-management controls" (id uc-4-capture-controls), stating what a
      user of the native app can now do that they could not: the app's FIRST controls that change server
      state, behind one ⋮ trigger at the header's trailing edge past the filters that scope them, beside a
      fixed-width slot reading the count those filters selected and reading "exporting…"/"importing…" while
      a transfer runs - the same box either way, so starting one moves nothing else in the row. A three-row
      table, one per act: export as har… gives the filtered capture through the OS save dialog under the
      filename THE SERVER stamped rather than one the app invented, and dismissing that dialog is an
      outcome not a failure (the line clears and nothing claims a file was written); import… is one modal
      taking either way in - a chosen .har file, or a curl command pasted into the target it opens focused
      on - whose refusal is the server's own sentence shown verbatim under the text that caused it with
      that text left in place to edit (links DEC-37); clear… is a modal ask titled with the count it will
      remove whose initial focus is on the answer that does nothing, with the consequence carrying the only
      alarm on the screen and the button carrying none. Then the two things the list itself gained: an
      imported row carries a ↑ mark in its time cell with its own accessible name, so a capture someone
      handed you is tellable from live traffic WITHOUT a column of its own (links DEC-36); and a cleared
      list gets a third absence of its own, "capture cleared", with the same remedy the never-captured one
      gives, because after a clear the remedy genuinely is the same and the heading is what says which
      nothing this is. Closes with the rule that binds all three: they refill the list from the event bus,
      never from their own response, because the responses carry counts rather than rows and two sources of
      truth for one list is a race - which is what the `cleared` event exists for. UC-6's and UC-7's
      provenance callouts each now name the route a person takes to what they already offered a client,
      replacing the placeholder both carried ("the app's own controls follow in the same epic") with a link
      to UC-4's block and the W6 badge. Header to v10 and the E-EXPORT attribution widened to name UC-4's
      capture-management controls.
  - path: docs/use-cases/use-cases-index.html
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: >-
      The UC-4 row says the surface is no longer read-only: behind one trigger in its header it takes the
      capture out, brings one back and clears it.
  - path: docs/work/2026-09-08-export-w6-controls-build-plan.html
    tier: T3
    action: added
    distills: >-
      The WU plan, written before the code - the requirement sentence, the mapping to the epic's W6 block
      with run 11 cited and run 10 explicitly not, a diagram of what gets built and where, the file-by-file
      diff with each file's design zone and an explicit "not built" line for the three proposals, six
      contract calls the run leaves to the build (how a second screen uses ModalShell without minting an
      entry, how F's textarea takes initial focus, where a refusal's text comes from, what tells the list
      it is empty BECAUSE it was cleared, whether the slot duplicates the dialog's in-flight line, and the
      cross-year overrun carried rather than fixed), eleven observables each with the test that proves it,
      the test plan per level with the e2e waived to the epic's own WebDriver row, and the distillation
      owed.
  - path: docs/work/2026-09-08-export-w6-controls-build-result.html
    tier: T3
    action: added
    distills: >-
      The WU result - delivered against all eleven observables; the shipped files with what was
      DELIBERATELY not built and why (no registry entry for Menu or Dialog, no GlyphName entry for ⋮ - all
      three are the run's proposals with named owners); six contract calls the build made, each with the
      reasoning a reviewer would otherwise have to reconstruct; three deviations (useConfirm carried as
      both design runs required and the epic plan's claim left wrong on the record; the WU plan's own
      observable 11 corrected because the e2e config inherits the window minimum rather than declaring one,
      with the guard asserting the condition under which the inheritance holds; and M's focus return being
      true by construction rather than by restoration, since DOM focus never leaves the trigger); one thing
      the build found - har_entry requires a response, so a request-only flow is invisible to the export
      while appearing in the listing, which is W2's deliberate contract and is recorded rather than worked
      around; the evidence table per check with per-suite counts; the distillation ledger including
      architecture.html re-read and NOT edited with the reason, and the rulebook/decisions deliberately
      untouched because this WU decided nothing run 11 had not; and four follow-ups - the epic's e2e, run
      11's second blocked-on carried forward with its owner unchanged, the three unpaid proposals now each
      proven in one call site, and the request-only/HAR fact.
note: >-
  Code and test files in this diff (ui/src/api.ts, CaptureControls.tsx, CaptureMenu.tsx, ModalShell.tsx,
  apiStub.ts, App.tsx, FlowList.tsx, ProxyCloseDialog.tsx, body.ts, src/api/mod.rs, xtask/src/ui.rs,
  tests/api.rs, and the two Tauri config files) carry no doc/v1 entry - they are T4, the truth the docs
  point at. T2 synthesis is UC-4's new controls block plus its index row (the what), with UC-6 and UC-7
  each gaining one cross-link where they previously deferred to a WU that had not happened yet. No new
  decision record and no rulebook change: every reason this WU could have written down was already
  decided and recorded by design run 11, and copying it down a tier is the duplication the tier system
  forbids - the result cites the run instead. T1 architecture.html was re-read at close and NOT edited:
  its UI seam says the native app is an out-of-process client of the control API, and a client that also
  POSTs and DELETEs is still exactly that; no store, module or event contract moved in this WU. One fact
  per tier held deliberately: the four request shapes live only in ui/src/api.ts and the use case
  describes them only by what a user gets; every word on the surface lives only in CAPTURE_COPY and the
  doc quotes three of them as the shape a person reads, never as the list; and the design's own reasoning
  (why one trigger, why 160px, why the ask focuses cancel) stays in run 11 and is cited from the result
  rather than restated in T2.
